@page "/help"
@attribute [Authorize]

<PageTitle>Help — vLLM Dashboard</PageTitle>

<h1 class="page-title">Help</h1>

<ul class="mb-4">
    <li><a href="#getting-started">Getting started</a></li>
    <li><a href="#how-it-works">How it works</a></li>
    <li><a href="#walkthrough">Walkthrough example</a></li>
    <li><a href="#troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</a></li>
</ul>

<div style="margin-bottom: var(--ui-space-4);" id="getting-started">
    <UiPanel Title="Getting started">
        <p>
            The dashboard doesn't start or stop containers itself — each machine runs its own
            <strong>supervisor</strong> (a small service alongside vLLM, started by your own compose file),
            and the dashboard talks to it over HTTP. Setting one up is three steps:
        </p>
        <ol>
            <li>
                <strong><a href="servers">Servers</a></strong> — register the machine (label, GPU info,
                models directory) and an instance on it (proxy slug, vLLM port, and the supervisor's own
                URL). Use <strong>[Test connection]</strong> before saving — it calls the supervisor
                directly and shows its GPU and current model, or a clear error if the URL is wrong or
                unreachable.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong><a href="models-recipes">Models &amp; Recipes</a></strong> — add a model (a local
                path, or download one from Hugging Face through the supervisor) and create a recipe for it
                (context length, GPU memory utilization, dtype, and the rest of vLLM's own launch flags).
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong><a href="">Overview</a></strong> — pick a recipe and swap it in. The swap ceremony
                shows a live diff, step-by-step progress as the supervisor reports it, and the result.
            </li>
        </ol>
        <p class="mb-0 text-muted">
            A fresh install shows a checklist on the Overview page for exactly these steps — it disappears
            for good after your first swap.
        </p>
    </UiPanel>
</div>

<div style="margin-bottom: var(--ui-space-4);" id="how-it-works">
    <UiPanel Title="How it works">
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>No hot-swap, idle-unload, or preload settings</strong> — those were part of an
                earlier design where the dashboard managed containers directly. Now the supervisor owns the
                vLLM process's whole lifecycle; the dashboard only ever asks it to start, stop, or restart
                a given recipe, and reads back its state.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Restore last known good model on boot</strong> — a per-instance toggle on the
                Servers page (once that instance's supervisor is registered). On, the supervisor
                automatically replays the last model that reached a healthy "ready" state when its
                container restarts. A run that failed or crashed is never replayed, and it gives up after 3
                attempts rather than retrying forever. Off, the instance just comes up idle and waits for
                you to swap something in.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>No token to manage</strong> — the supervisor's admin API binds to that machine's own
                loopback address, not the network, so there's nothing for the dashboard to authenticate with
                beyond reaching it at all. Run the dashboard and supervisor so they share that loopback (e.g.
                <code>network_mode: host</code>) rather than exposing the admin API on the LAN.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Changing an instance's proxy slug changes its URL</strong> — every client pointed at
                <code>/proxy/&lt;slug&gt;/v1</code> needs updating if you rename it. The Servers page warns
                you when you do this; it still saves the change.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </UiPanel>
</div>

<div style="margin-bottom: var(--ui-space-4);" id="walkthrough">
    <UiPanel Title="Walkthrough example">
        <p>A concrete example of what to enter on the Servers page for a typical single-box vLLM setup:</p>
        <div class="ui-table-wrap" style="margin-bottom: var(--ui-space-4);">
            <table class="ui-table">
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td class="text-muted">Server label</td><td>My GPU box</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="text-muted">GPU name</td><td>e.g. <code>NVIDIA RTX 4090</code>, or anything containing "Intel" for the Intel dtype rule</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="text-muted">Models directory</td><td><code>/models</code> — shared by every instance on this server</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="text-muted">Instance label</td><td>primary</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="text-muted">Proxy slug</td><td><code>primary</code> — becomes <code>/proxy/primary/v1</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="text-muted">vLLM port</td><td><code>9999</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="text-muted">Supervisor URL</td><td><code>http://127.0.0.1:&lt;admin-port&gt;</code> — loopback, not the LAN address</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p>Then on <a href="models-recipes">Models &amp; Recipes</a>, for a Hugging Face model:</p>
        <div class="ui-table-wrap">
            <table class="ui-table">
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td class="text-muted">Repo id</td><td><code>meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="text-muted">Served model name</td><td><code>llama-3.1-8b</code> — whatever your API clients should request; shown again on the Instances page's client snippet</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="text-muted">Max model length</td><td>Set explicitly rather than leaving blank — the editor shows the model's own reported default as you type</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    </UiPanel>
</div>

<div id="troubleshooting">
    <UiPanel Title="Troubleshooting">
        <ul class="mb-0">
            <li>
                <strong>"Test connection" fails</strong> — confirm the supervisor URL is reachable from this
                dashboard (not just from your own machine). Since the admin API only listens on loopback,
                this almost always means the dashboard and supervisor containers aren't sharing a network
                namespace (e.g. one isn't on <code>network_mode: host</code>) rather than a firewall issue.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>An instance shows "needs supervisor registration"</strong> — it exists in the
                dashboard (often from an earlier version's data) but has no supervisor URL saved yet.
                Its proxy URL keeps forwarding traffic either way; only swapping is blocked until you
                register it on the Servers page.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>A model loads but uses far more VRAM than expected</strong> — check whether GPU
                memory utilization and max model length are set explicitly on the recipe. Left at their
                defaults, vLLM may fall back to the model's own (sometimes very large) native context length.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>A swap seems stuck</strong> — the first load of an uncached Hugging Face model
                downloads its weights first, which can take a long time on a slow connection; the swap
                ceremony's progress steps and live log view show exactly where it is. If it's genuinely
                stuck, Cancel is always available and leaves the instance in a known state (serving the old
                model, or idle, depending on how far the swap got).
            </li>
        </ul>
    </UiPanel>
</div>
